ProbabilityTheory.hasCondDistrib_pi_of_h:
ProbabilityTheory.hasCondDistrib_pi_of_hasCondDistrib_const.{u_1, u_2, u_4} {Ω : Type u_1}
  {β : Type u_2} {𝓩 : Type u_4} {mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω} {mβ : MeasurableSpace β}
  {m𝓩 : MeasurableSpace 𝓩} {P : MeasureTheory.Measure Ω} {ν : MeasureTheory.Measure β}
  [MeasureTheory.IsProbabilityMeasure P] [MeasureTheory.IsProbabilityMeasure ν] {W : ℕ → Ω → β}
  {Z : Ω → 𝓩} (hZ : AEMeasurable Z P)
  (h :
    ∀ (k : ℕ),
      ProbabilityTheory.HasCondDistrib (W k) (fun ω => (Z ω, fun i => W (↑i) ω))
        (ProbabilityTheory.Kernel.const (𝓩 × (Fin k → β)) ν) P)
  (n : ℕ) :
  ProbabilityTheory.HasCondDistrib (fun ω i => W (↑i) ω) Z
    (ProbabilityTheory.Kernel.const 𝓩 (MeasureTheory.Measure.pi fun x => ν)) P

**A sequence with a constant conditional law given `Z` is i.i.d. and independent of `Z`**:
if for every `k` the conditional law of `W k` given `(Z, W 0, …, W (k - 1))` is the constant `ν`,
then the conditional law of `(W 0, …, W (n - 1))` given `Z` is the constant product law `ν^n`.

